
#!/bin/bash

# This script sets environment variables for the project.
# It should be sourced, not executed, to ensure the variables are set in the current shell.
# Usage:
#  source infra/setenv.sh
#  infra/deploy.sh ...

TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=
TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID=

# --- Provider API keys: NOT deploy variables any more -------------------------
# add-user-provider-keys (BREAKING): the four provider API keys used to live
# here and were injected into the runtime environment at deploy time — one
# shared secret for every user, every workspace and every session, rotatable
# only with a redeploy (which resets the session storage of every session).
#
# They are now a per-USER property. Each user creates their own file, outside any
# repository, readable only by them:
#
#   mkdir -p ~/.sch && touch ~/.sch/env && chmod 600 ~/.sch/env
#   cat > ~/.sch/env <<'EOF'
#   ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
#   OPENCODE_API_KEY=
#   OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
#   KILO_API_KEY=
#   EOF
#
# `sch` reads that file at every command and forwards only those four names in
# the invoke payload; the shim stages them on tmpfs for the session and the
# harness dispatcher maps them onto the canonical provider names exactly as
# before (additive for opencode/pi, Bedrock -> Anthropic API switch for claude).
# See docs/cli.md, "Provider API keys (per-user)".
#
# Nothing else changes here: the deployment still owns the Telegram credentials
# above (and any other `deploy.sh` variable) — those ARE per-deployment. Provider
# key variables left over in this file are simply ignored by deploy.sh.
